<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sambre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sambre is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, flowing about 190 km north-east to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> at Namur. It is usually identified with the Sabis of Caesar's account, where the Nervii were defeated in 57 BCE. It later flowed through the counties of Hainaut and Namur and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> region of Charleroi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sambre.jpg" alt="Sambre" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Namur_JPG02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Sambre is a river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and a left-bank tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, which it joins at Namur, the Walloon capital. Its source lies near Le Nouvion-en-Thiérache, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a> department. In France it passes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landrecies">Landrecies</a>, Aulnoye-Aymeries, Hautmont and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maubeuge">Maubeuge</a> in the Nord department, and in Belgium Thuin, Montigny-le-Tilleul, Charleroi, Floreffe and Namur.</p><p>Its main tributaries include the Helpe Mineure, the Helpe Majeure, the Eau d'Heure, which gives its name to the Eau d'Heure lakes, the Piéton, which joins in Charleroi, and the Orneau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river runs through the Franco-Belgian coal basin, formerly an important industrial district. It lies at the western end of the sillon industriel, which remains the backbone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a>'s industry even though all coal mining has ceased and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry has declined.</p><p>Navigation begins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landrecies">Landrecies</a>, where the Canal de la Sambre à l'Oise joins the river and links it with the central French waterway network, until navigation was interrupted in 2006 following structural failures. The French stretch runs 54 km with nine locks down to the border at Jeumont. Beyond the border the river is canalised in two sections over 88 km with 17 locks. The Haute-Sambre is 39 km long, with ten locks, down to Charleroi, and the rest of the Belgian river was upgraded to European Class IV dimensions, for barges of 1,350 tonnes, in the years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p><p>Fleurus, a suburb of Charleroi on the north bank, saw three important battles: the Thirty Years' War Battle of Fleurus in 1622, the Nine Years' War Battle of Fleurus in 1690 and, on 26 June 1794, the Battle of Fleurus, the most significant battle of the Flanders Campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>. The last was fought on both banks of the river after a campaign of many crossings and re-crossings. Heavy fighting also occurred along the river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, at the siege of Namur in 1914, during the Battle of Charleroi, and in the Battle of the Sambre in 1918.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sambre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hainaut took its name from the Haine, a tributary of the Scheldt, and grew out of a Carolingian district (pagus) between the Scheldt and the <b>Sambre</b> in the old Frankish heartland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Namur emerged in the 10th century from the old district of Lomme, when local counts built their power around the castle on the rock above the <b>Sambre</b> and Meuse confluence.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-namur">County of Namur</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nervii lived in the Hainaut region of modern Belgium and northern France, between the Scheldt and the <b>Sambre</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/nervii">Nervii</a></li></ul></section>
